<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Offline AI Chat</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>offline_ai_chat</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleSignature</key>
	<string>????</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>

	<!--
		Deliberately absent: the model-sideloading keys. Four keys used to live here and all
		four existed for one feature — hand-importing a GGUF — which no longer exists. The
		catalog download in the model manager is now the only way a model reaches the device,
		so each of them was a promise the app can no longer keep:

		  CFBundleDocumentTypes / UTExportedTypeDeclarations (net.salebee.gguf)
		      Declared the app as the owner-rank handler for .gguf, which is what made Files,
		      Mail and AirDrop offer "Open in Offline AI Chat". There is nothing left to open
		      into: the app has no `application(_:open:options:)` handler (see
		      AppDelegate.swift) and no importer behind it, so accepting the open would dead-end.

		  UIFileSharingEnabled
		      Exposed Documents/ in Files and over iTunes/Finder sharing, purely so a user
		      could drop a multi-gigabyte GGUF in and then pick it. It does not expose
		      downloaded models: those live in Library/Application Support/Models, which file
		      sharing never shows. Nothing else of this app's writes to Documents/ either —
		      SqfliteConversationRepository deliberately puts the chat database in Application
		      Support rather than sqflite's default Documents path *because* this key would
		      expose it. Leaving it on would publish an empty, user-writable folder and re-arm
		      that trap for the next thing that reaches for a default path.

		  LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace
		      Only meaningful next to a document type: it says the app can be handed a URL to a
		      file that stays where the user put it. With the .gguf declaration gone nothing
		      hands this app a URL at all.

		Attaching documents to a chat is unaffected and needs none of these. It runs through
		file_picker -> UIDocumentPickerViewController, which grants access per pick with no
		Info.plist declaration and no permission prompt, and copies the file into the app's own
		sandbox before returning a path — so there is no security-scoped resource to open and
		nothing here to declare. See lib/agent/documents/local_document_manager.dart.
	-->

	<!--
		Portrait plus both landscapes on iPhone; everything on iPad. Generation is not
		interrupted by a rotation — the engine runs on its own isolate and the UI rebuild
		is independent of it.
	-->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>

	<!--
		UIBackgroundModes is deliberately absent, and this is a design decision rather than
		an oversight.

		Inference is foreground-only. A background mode would be needed to keep generating
		after the user leaves the app, and none of the available modes honestly describe
		what this app does: there is no audio, no location, no VoIP, and `processing` /
		`fetch` are for short, system-scheduled, deferrable work — not for a 2 GB resident
		process burning the CPU for a minute. Requesting one to keep a token stream alive
		is exactly the abuse App Review rejects, and on a memory-pressured device the app
		would be the first thing killed anyway.

		The consequence, which the UI has to handle rather than hide: backgrounding the app
		mid-generation suspends the isolate, and iOS may terminate the process while
		suspended. The engine's cancellation flag makes that a clean stop rather than a
		corrupt KV cache.
	-->

	<!--
		NSAppTransportSecurity is deliberately absent too. Absent means ATS defaults:
		TLS 1.2+, no arbitrary loads, no cleartext HTTP. Web search and model download are
		the only network callers and both are HTTPS. Adding even an empty dict here starts
		a conversation with App Review that this app has no reason to have — this mirrors
		android:usesCleartextTraffic="false" on the other platform.

		No usage-description strings are present either (no NSCameraUsageDescription,
		NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription, NSMicrophoneUsageDescription, …): document import
		goes through UIDocumentPickerViewController, which grants access per-file without
		a permission prompt, and nothing else touches a protected resource.
	-->

	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSupportsIndirectInputEvents</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
